Describe the flow of HTTPS requests through a Spring Boot application.
-->
1. Client initiates HTTPS request

Browser / client sends an HTTPS request (TLS encrypted) to:
https://api.example.com

Request includes:
URL
Headers
Encrypted payload

2. TLS/SSL handshake (Transport layer)
Happens before Spring Boot is involved
Managed by:
Embedded server (Tomcat / Jetty / Undertow)
Or external load balancer / reverse proxy

Steps:

Server presents SSL certificate
Client validates certificate
Encryption keys are negotiated
After handshake → secure TCP channel established

✅ At this point, HTTP data is decrypted for the server

3. Embedded Servlet container (Tomcat)

Tomcat:
Accepts decrypted HTTP request
Converts it into HttpServletRequest
Hands request to the Servlet filter chain

4. Servlet Filters (including Spring Security)

Filters execute before request reaches Spring MVC
Common filters:
SecurityFilterChain (authentication, authorization)
CORS filter
Logging / tracing filters
Multipart filter (file uploads)
Example:
Client → Tomcat → Filters → DispatcherServlet

5. DispatcherServlet (Front Controller)

Central entry point for Spring MVC
Responsibilities:
Finds appropriate controller using HandlerMapping
Delegates execution to handler (controller)

6. Handler Interceptors (optional)

Execute before and after controller
Used for:
Auditing
Metrics
Custom authorization logic

7. Controller layer

Request is mapped to:
@GetMapping("/users/{id}")
Spring:
Binds path variables, request params, body
Performs validation
Converts JSON → Java objects

8. Service layer

Business logic execution
Transaction management
Calls repositories or external services

9. Repository / Data access layer

Interacts with database via:
JPA / Hibernate
JDBC
Returns domain objects

10. Response processing

Controller returns:
Object
ResponseEntity
Spring:
Serializes object → JSON (Jackson)
Sets HTTP status and headers

11. Response filters & interceptors

Post-processing logic:
Security headers
Logging
Metrics

12. Response sent back to client

Tomcat:
Encrypts HTTP response using TLS
Client receives secure HTTPS response

Key interview insights (experienced)

TLS termination may happen:
At Tomcat
OR at Load Balancer (AWS ALB, Nginx)
Spring Boot handles HTTP, not encryption directly
DispatcherServlet is the heart of request routing
Filters run before interceptors
HTTPS adds transport-level security, not application logic

One-line interview summary

“An HTTPS request is decrypted by the server after TLS(Transport layer security) handshake, processed through servlet filters, routed by DispatcherServlet to controllers,
 handled by business layers,then to repository layer then response processing happens and  then encrypted again and sent back to the client.”


