How to test spring boot application
-->
1.Unit Testing

Unit tests focus on testing individual components (like services or repositories) in isolation, often using mock objects.

Tools:
JUnit 5 (test framework)
Mockito (mocking dependencies)
AssertJ (fluent assertions)

Example:
@SpringBootTest
class UserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    void testFindUserById() {
        User user = new User(1L, "Alice");
        Mockito.when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(user));

        User result = userService.findUserById(1L);

        Assertions.assertEquals("Alice", result.getName());
    }
}


✅ Key points:

Use @Mock to mock dependencies.
Use @InjectMocks to inject mocks into the class under test.
Keep tests small and fast.


2. Integration Testing

Integration tests check how multiple components work together, often loading the Spring context.

Annotations:
@SpringBootTest → loads the entire Spring context
@AutoConfigureMockMvc → sets up MockMvc for testing controllers
@DataJpaTest → tests repositories with an in-memory database (like H2)

Example: Testing a REST Controller
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
class UserControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    void testGetUser() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/users/1"))
               .andExpect(status().isOk())
               .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Alice"));
    }
}


✅ Key points:
Use MockMvc to simulate HTTP requests without starting a server.
For database tests, H2 is commonly used to avoid touching production data.

1. Unit Testing:
For unit tests, I focus on testing individual components like services or utilities in isolation. 
I use JUnit 5 for writing tests and Mockito to mock dependencies. This ensures that my business logic works correctly 
without involving external dependencies. For example, I mock repositories while testing service classes.

2. Integration Testing:
Integration tests are crucial to verify that multiple components work together correctly. In Spring Boot,
 I use @SpringBootTest to load the application context, @AutoConfigureMockMvc to test REST controllers, and 
 @DataJpaTest for testing repository layers with an in-memory database like H2. I also ensure that database transactions are 
 rolled back after each test to maintain test isolation.

3. End-to-End Testing:
For critical workflows, I write end-to-end tests using tools like RestAssured or Testcontainers to test the 
application against real databases or external services. This helps catch integration issues that unit or integration tests might miss.



