Explain Spring Boot's approach to handling asynchronous operations.
-->
1.Using @Async:
Spring Boot provides simple async execution using the @Async annotation. When a method is annotated with @Async, 
it runs in a separate thread managed by a Spring TaskExecutor, allowing the caller thread to continue without blocking.

With @Async, methods can return both Future and CompletableFuture
Future is a basic handle to an async result that often forces blocking, 
whereas CompletableFuture provides a powerful, non-blocking, composable API for building complex asynchronous workflows.

@Async
public CompletableFuture<String> processAsync() {
    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture("Done");
}


To enable this, we use:
@EnableAsync
We place @EnableAsync on a configuration or main application class so Spring can detect and execute



