What is the N+1 Query Problem? (simple definition)

N+1 queries happen when your code executes 1 query to fetch a list, and then N additional queries — 
one for each item in the list.

N+1 queries occur when one query fetches a list and then N additional queries fetch related data for each item. 
It causes performance issues and is common in ORM-based applications. It can be fixed using join fetches, batch fetching, or optimized queries.


example
Scenario
You want all Orders and their Customers
❌ Bad approach (N+1)
List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll(); // 1 query

for (Order order : orders) {
    Customer c = customerRepo.findById(order.getCustomerId());
}

SQL executed
1 query  → SELECT * FROM orders;
N queries → SELECT * FROM customer WHERE id = ?;


If:
1,000 orders → 1,001 queries

100,000 orders → 💥

📉 Why this is bad

Massive DB round-trips

Slow response times

High DB load

Scales terribly

Often works fine in local (small data)
Breaks badly in production

🧪 Real JPA / Hibernate Example
List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();

for (Order order : orders) {
    System.out.println(order.getCustomer().getName());
}


If customer is LAZY:

1 query for orders

1 query per order for customer

👉 Classic N+1.

✅ How to detect N+1

Enable SQL logs

Look for repeated similar queries

Use tools:

Hibernate statistics

APM (New Relic, Datadog)

DB slow query logs

🛠️ How to fix N+1 (MOST IMPORTANT)
1️⃣ Join fetch (JPA / Hibernate)
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.customer")
List<Order> findAllWithCustomer();


One query. Done.


